{:edb/db-before-t 1219 :edb/db-after-t 1220 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534532 50 #inst "2026-10-12T02:13:02.482Z" 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186119824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424436 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186119825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424437 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186351160 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}={b}_{1}?$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186351160 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}={b}_{1}?$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 false] [17592186351164 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}?$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186351164 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}?$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 false] [17592186351164 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=4(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-7{b}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7({c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7{c}_{1}+14{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+\\frac{29}{2}{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186351164 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=4(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-7{b}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7({c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7{c}_{1}+14{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+\\frac{29}{2}{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 false] [17592186380636 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=3{b}_{2}?$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186380636 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=3{b}_{2}?$" 13194139534532 17592186045417 false] [17592186424423 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424438 13194139534532 17592186045417 true] [17592186424423 1043 #e